#. §0: `¬`
#. §1: `max a (-a) = 1`
#. §2: `a`
#. §3: `decide`
#: Game/Levels/TruthTables/L05_ExcludedMiddle.lean:8
msgid "*Either the king of France is bald or he is not.* Classically every instance of *p or not p* is\n"
"true. Trivalently some instance is not, which is what the statement below says: it is not the\n"
"case (§0) that §1 for every §2. §3 checks negated and quantified statements over\n"
"finitely many values as well."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/Laws/L04_DoubleNegation.lean:20
msgid "*It is not the case that the king of France is not bald* has the value of *the king of France is\n"
"bald*: double negation holds in Strong Kleene logic, and the presupposition survives two negations\n"
"as it survives one."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `1`
#. §1: `0`
#. §2: `0`
#. §3: `rfl`
#. §4: `rfl`
#: Game/Levels/TruthTables/L01_TrueAndUndefined.lean:8
msgid "*Paris is in France and the king of France is bald.* The first conjunct is true and the second is\n"
"undefined. In Strong Kleene logic the conjunction is undefined, since the minimum of §0 and §1\n"
"is §2.\n"
"\n"
"The goal below is an equation that holds by computation: both sides evaluate to the same value.\n"
"The tactic §3 closes such a goal. Type §4 and press Execute."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `-1`
#. §1: `-1`
#: Game/Levels/TruthTables/L02_FalseAndUndefined.lean:8
msgid "*Paris is in Spain and the king of France is bald.* Now the first conjunct is false, and Strong\n"
"Kleene makes the conjunction false whatever the value of the second: the minimum of §0 and any\n"
"value is §1."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/TruthTables/L03_Negation.lean:8
msgid "*The king of France is not bald.* Negating an undefined sentence leaves it undefined. This is the\n"
"familiar observation that presuppositions survive negation."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `↔`
#. §1: `∨`
#: Game/Levels/TruthTables/L06_FalseConjunctions.lean:8
msgid "A Strong Kleene conjunction is false exactly when one of its conjuncts is false. The statement\n"
"uses §0 for *if and only if* and §1 for *or*."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/TruthTables/L06_FalseConjunctions.lean:13
msgid "A conjunction is false iff one of its conjuncts is false."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/OrderMatters/L06_ThreeConjunctions.lean:29
msgid "A conjunction that is undefined more often projects more presuppositions. Read as theories of\n"
"projection, Weak Kleene predicts that the presupposition of either conjunct always projects,\n"
"Middle Kleene that the left conjunct can satisfy the presupposition of the right one, and Strong\n"
"Kleene that either conjunct can satisfy the presupposition of the other.\n"
"\n"
"Theories of projection can be ordered in the same way, by how strong the presuppositions they\n"
"predict are. The next world does this for the theories in the literature."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/OrderMatters/L03_Counterexample.lean:6
msgid "A counterexample"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `¬ a.Stronger b`
